[22:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with River in various different ways. And that\'s kind of true of a lot of uh a lot of things with Freenet. There\'s kind of a friendly web user interface, but we'

[22:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'also make available command line tools so that everything can be automated and scripted. So,'

[23:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Freenet is almost we think about Freenet almost like an operating system. And so, um when we\'re designing Freenet, it\'s it\'s implemented in Rust. So, it\'s kind'

[23:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of a low low-level language, a systems programming language uh that allows you to allows your code to be very very efficient. uh it\'s very good in terms of'

[23:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'concurrency and managing thread pools and all of that. So um Freenet is really built kind of like an operating system in that every every aspect of it we\'ve tried to'

[23:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'optimize it as much as possible. Certainly far from perfect yet. Um but the way we think about it is really in terms of h how do we how do we make this as efficient as it can possibly be?'

[23:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And so one example of how we do this. So an important part of what Freenet peers are actually doing typically is they are helping other peers find contracts on'

[24:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the network. So my peer if it\'s looking for a particular contract it it\'s connected to a bunch of other peers in the network and it will determine it\'ll say well okay which of these other peers'

[24:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is going to help me find this contract fastest and the I I almost compare this to to the idea of branch prediction'

[24:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'um in uh CPU design. So the way that the way that Freenet solves this problem is it actually treats it as a supervised learning problem with machine learning.'

[24:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And we use a uh supervised learning algorithm that I call Renegade that\'s uh it\'s been open source for years. Uh also implemented in Rust. Uh, Renegade is a k'

[25:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'nearest neighbors um, uh, learning algorithm with a learned metric. So what the what that means is that it it'

[25:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'basically works by if it\'s trying to make a prediction about what will happen in a certain set of circumstances, it will look at past circumstances that were similar in order to uh, predict what will happen in the future. because'

[25:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'often, not always, but often the past is the best predictor of of the future. And the reason that I went with K nearest neighbors is that it\'s it\'s an'

[25:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'algorithm that does really really well at making accurate predictions even when it\'s got a relatively small amount of data. So the idea is that even when a'

[25:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'freenet pier first starts up, it doesn\'t have any data, but then as it interacts with its neighbors, it starts to learn, oh, you know that this this neighbor is really good at finding this contract or'

[26:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'uh it\'ll kind of try to find patterns that will allow it to predict which of its neighbors will uh will be able to find the contract it\'s looking for'

[26:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fastest. And so you can kind of think of Freenet almost like u uh this global network of learning peers that are constantly'

[26:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'learning how to find things more quickly and also adapting to changing circumstances. So let\'s let\'s say if uh let\'s say if a particular I don\'t know may maybe a'

[26:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'undersea cable uh goes down or gets degraded and certain peers that were really fast in the past are now slower. Uh Renegade will figure that out very'

[27:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'quickly and the uh individual freet peers will adapt to that in in their rooting uh very quickly. And if any of you need a uh good generalpurpose'

[27:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'supervised learning algorithm, uh Renegade, I think it\'s under the LGPL license. Um and it\'s implemented in Rust and you\'re welcome to use it. My my'

[27:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'other design goal with Renegade is I just wanted a supervised learning algorithm that you didn\'t have to tune that it just performed well with the default settings and you don\'t need to'

[27:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mess around with uh uh multivariate testing and and all of this kind of thing to get it working well. Um it\'s it\'s fast and it just works the first time and it\'s very efficient. uh'

[27:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'particularly it\'s very data efficient which would contrast it with something like a neural network where you need vast amounts of data to train them and that just wouldn\'t fly in in this circumstance.'

