[29:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'videos. I\'m I\'m all cute, puppied out. Um, and so it it really empowers the user. And then, as I mentioned earlier, we\'ve also uh built a conduit for git that allows people to publish git'

[29:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'repositories on freenet. So, yeah. So, so River um I don\'t know has anyone has anyone tried it yet? Oh, hundreds of hands have gone up here.'

[30:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Um, so River is a a pretty standard group chat. Um, if if you um if you join it, the user interface will will be very familiar. Um,'

[30:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and of course because it\'s built on Freenet, there\'s no there\'s no company behind it. The the everything about the system is decentralized. uh each room on freet each room on river'

[30:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'uh is its own freet contract. So they\'re they\'re uh it\'s not all stored in the same place. And uh River\'s actually been up for a'

[30:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'while. Like we\'ve had people using it for months at this point. But what\'s new is that uh we\'ve supported we\'ve added support for private chat rooms. Um, previously it was kind of more like uh'

[30:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'an IRC type thing where where the assumption was that that rooms were fairly public. Um, but uh we now have private rooms. So all messages and uh most of the information in the room is'

[31:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'encrypted using a symmetric encryption algorithm. Um the room owner uh determines what that encryption key is and then shares it'

[31:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with all of the members of the room uh using asymmetric cryptography uh to encrypt it so only they can read it. And then we rotate that key. It it it\'s'

[31:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'rotated once a week normally or if anyone is is banned from the room uh then that encryption key is is rotated. Um'

[31:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and so yeah so so uh through this encryption it enables private rooms and we also support direct messages which between users which are also encrypted. Um so but I want to be clear about'

[32:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'exactly what uh river uh does do and doesn\'t do. So it\'s encrypting the message content. Uh it\'s uh it encrypts the kind of some room metadata like the'

[32:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'room uh description and uh obviously if you\'re not a member then you don\'t have the the room symmetric key so you can\'t uh read any of this stuff and if you get kicked out'

[32:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of the room for whatever reason uh then the symmetric key will be is rotated so you can\'t read anything going forward. But there are um you do need there are constraints that are imposed by the way'

[32:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'freeet works. So uh it doesn\'t hide the fact that a room exists. So people can see the room contract on the network. They can\'t really see any of the'

[32:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'messages within the room because they\'re all uh encrypted. But you can see things like the timing of messages and you could guess at guess at the message size. Um, and you can also you can see'

[33:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'how many members the room has. Uh, but you can\'t you can\'t see their uh you can\'t see their kind of friendly names because those are encrypted. Um, but'

[33:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it\'s kind of a mixture. It\'s not uh uh there is some information that\'s probably less important does leak. Um and in particular uh so something we'

[33:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'don\'t if you look at signal which is you know the uh uh probably the first thing people think of when they think of uh privacy preserving group chat. So,'

[33:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'signal does something uh has a mechanism called a double ratchet and the this is really geared around the idea of what happens if the private symmetric key'

[34:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'leaks um is somebody going to be able to read every message that\'s ever been sent to the room or are they going to be able to see every read every future message that\'s sent to the room? And the idea of'

[34:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this double ratchet mechanism is that it means that if the message leaks if if the private if the uh symmetric key leaks then people can only'

[34:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'uh can only read that one message and they can\'t then use that symmetric key to go and read all past messages or all future messages. uh River uh River takes uh uh I would say a less of aggressive'

[34:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'approach. So if the if the private key leaks and somebody gets it that shouldn\'t have it, they will be able to read messages up until when that key is rotated, which could be uh up to a week.'

[34:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Uh so I think it\'s I think it\'s important to to kind of be transparent about this kind of limitation although I would argue that the things that you really care about being encrypted uh'

[35:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'river is encrypting. So, Delta uh which is which is our kind of Squarespace or or um uh it\'s just it allows you to build websites in markdown'

[35:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and then you can have hyperlinks between these pages. Um and so it\'s pretty simple. You can embed images, that type of thing. Um it\'s very good if you want to create a blog or something like that.'

[35:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Um, and there\'s also a DeltaCTL. So, there\'s a command line tool, so you can completely automate Delta. Uh, you can have your AI agent update it or or do whatever you need to do. So, it\'s it\'s'

[35:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it\'s very flexible, but also uh very easy to use and of course completely decentralized. So, Atlas, which is our uh discovery'

[36:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'search engine, um the problem of discovery, the problem of how people find stuff on the internet, I I think is it\'s critical. And one of the things'

[36:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that makes it critical is that if the recommendation engine, if the algorithms that are being used to decide what you see are not transparent, then it\'s very easy to manipulate people. It\'s very'

[36:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'easy to hide things from people and it\'s it\'s just very very dangerous. And yet that\'s how everything works. Nobody outside Google really knows how the Google algorithm works. Um, no. And if'

[36:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Google is pressured by governments to manipulate that algorithm, there\'s really no easy way for for people to know. So, this is extremely dangerous.'

[36:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And I think decentralizing how we find information, how we\'re exposed to new information is critical. And that\'s exactly what what Atlas does. Um, so with that, but at the same time,'

[37:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you want to make it flexible. You want to empower the user, but the user shouldn\'t need a PhD in order to use the the search engine or the recommendation'

[37:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'engine. So we we provide intelligent defaults. So if you were to go to Atlas on Freenet today, um you would uh it we have a default crawler which just kind'

[37:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of crawls everything on Freenet and makes it available and then we use a a large language model to come up with to create a uh neutral'

[37:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'uh description of uh the whatever is being linked on atlas. So the beauty of that is that it kind of t the whole SEO thing where people are kind of trying to'

[38:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'manipulate you into visiting your website based upon the snippets that appear on Google. That\'s all irrelevant because a large language model is just describing what these things are in a'

[38:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'neutral way and it\'s just a lot more uh uh pleasant. Um, and of course you don\'t have to. Maybe you don\'t like large language mo, maybe you don\'t like AI, maybe you don\'t like large language'

[38:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'models. You don\'t need to use that. Uh, the beauty of of Atlas is that it\'s really up to you. So you can decide which crawlers you trust. You can decide which labelers you trust to characterize'

[38:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the information that you\'re finding uh on Atlas. Uh so freenet git this is uh uh pretty simple and actually I was surprised by'

[38:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'how well it worked. Um but you can install freenet git I think it\'s just the rep githubfreenetfreenet.git git. Um, and it allows you to publish a Git repository in Freenet completely'

